00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:29.208
FELIPE HOFFA: Hi, Mark.

00:09:29.208 --> 00:09:32.500
So what we have here is a
Stack Overflow Predictor.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.260
How long will it take for
people to answer your question?

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
does that mean specifically?

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:40.210
So I can go into Stack
Overflow, I can ask a question,

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:42.560
but someone's not going
to respond immediately.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:43.352
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:09:43.352 --> 00:09:45.152
So I don't know
if you ever asked

00:09:45.152 --> 00:09:46.360
a question of Stack Overflow.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:48.295
It takes some
time, and you never

00:09:48.295 --> 00:09:49.420
know how long will it take.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:49.870
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:09:49.870 --> 00:09:51.328
FELIPE HOFFA: Well,
with this tool,

00:09:51.328 --> 00:09:53.230
we can kind of predict
how long will it take,

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.570
looking at all the
past data that we

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:57.100
have from Stack Overflow.

00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:58.180
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Now,
how do you do that?

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:00.530
Because BigQuery, you can
do a bunch of data analysis.

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:02.410
But you can't build a machine
learning model in BigQuery,

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:02.910
right?

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:04.270
FELIPE HOFFA: Well, you can now.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:05.680
So I love BigQuery.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:07.360
I love Data Analytics.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:08.890
Thanks to the
public data program,

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:11.920
we have a copy of Stack
Overflow, the dump that they

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:13.730
publish every quarter.

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:15.590
So we loaded that into
Query, and then you

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:18.080
can start running your
own SQL queries over it.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:20.660
Anyone can come and run
any queries they want.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:23.100
They have a free
terabyte every month.

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:25.520
So the first step
with that analytics

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.830
is just to look at
all your questions,

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:29.210
look at all your
answers, and get

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:32.660
an average of how long did
it take for people to answer

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:33.827
questions on Stack Overflow.

00:10:33.827 --> 00:10:36.410
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So people can
come here to the Next Showcase

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.030
floor, play around
with this interface

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:40.505
here, change and see
if they ask a question

00:10:40.505 --> 00:10:42.380
and then based on the
machine learning model,

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:45.017
they'll see about how long
they'd have to wait for someone

00:10:45.017 --> 00:10:46.100
to go ahead and answer it.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:47.017
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:10:47.017 --> 00:10:50.340
So how did we go from the
analytics of just average group

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:52.550
but to a prediction?

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:54.530
There's a lot of
spaces in between.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:59.510
Like, for example, to get an
answer for the Google BigQuery

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:03.320
tag at 8:00 AM for a
medium question that

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:05.960
ends with-- doesn't end with
a question mark, that starts

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:10.280
with a Y, for someone that
created an account in 2017,

00:11:10.280 --> 00:11:14.180
we're predicting that
it will take 53 minutes.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:15.800
Maybe they will
not get an answer,

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:19.540
but with an 85% probability,
they will get one.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:24.770
And there is a chance of
being down-voted of about 7%

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:25.980
for these statistics.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:28.190
Now, how did we build this?

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:31.400
We used-- we created a
linear regression using

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.775
all of these features and the
data that we had available.

00:11:33.775 --> 00:11:36.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So with all
this different information,

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.600
you can know when you ask
a question, approximately,

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:41.170
hopefully pretty relatively,
when it's going to be answered,

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:42.590
but you could also
maybe even predict

00:11:42.590 --> 00:11:44.060
when's the best time
to ask a question.

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:44.660
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.170
If you want to go
the opposite way.

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:50.110
So let's say it takes
53 minutes on a Tuesday.

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:54.890
On Sunday, it takes more like
an hour to get an answer.

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.515
But there is a higher
chance of getting an answer.

00:11:57.515 --> 00:11:59.390
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
lots of cool information

00:11:59.390 --> 00:12:00.530
to play around with here.

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:02.300
But people can also
come here and actually

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:03.933
learn BQML hands on, right?

00:12:03.933 --> 00:12:04.850
FELIPE HOFFA: Exactly.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:07.940
So the alternatives for
people that are not here,

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:11.570
to see how I made this, they can
go to my blog post on Medium,

00:12:11.570 --> 00:12:15.280
just search for Felipe Hoffa,
when will Stack Overflow reply?

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.870
And if you are here at Next,
we have code labs ready

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:21.200
for you to run that you
can also find online.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.960
But there are so many
ways to learn and get

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:25.397
your hands into these tools.

00:12:25.397 --> 00:12:26.480
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Perfect.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:28.460
So lots of cool stuff from BQML.

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:30.980
Come down to the floor,
get started, or check out

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:33.608
the resources online.

00:12:33.608 --> 00:12:34.400
NATALIE PIUCCO: Hi.

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:37.730
I'm Natalie, and we are in
the Intelligence Neighborhood

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:39.470
in the Sounds and Models demo.

00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:41.840
And I'm joined here by Yufeng.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:44.420
Yufeng, tell us a little
bit about what you do

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:46.790
and this awesome demo
that you've created.

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:49.260
YUFENG GUO: Here, we have
Station 1, gathering data.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.652
And we have this kind of
instrument, if you will.

00:12:51.652 --> 00:12:53.110
But it's actually
five instruments.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.318
And so each string represents
a different instrument.

00:12:55.318 --> 00:12:59.010
And we can pluck our string
to make different sounds.

00:12:59.010 --> 00:13:01.490
And what we're going
to do is use this

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:03.770
as our kind of data source.

00:13:03.770 --> 00:13:07.550
The sounds are going to
then go into Dataflow

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.530
and get converted
into spectrograms.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:12.560
They're going to get moved
into the frequency space.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:15.890
And those images can then
be used to train our model.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:18.860
The hope is that these images
will be distinct enough

00:13:18.860 --> 00:13:20.928
to be able to--

00:13:20.928 --> 00:13:22.220
NATALIE PIUCCO: Represent each.

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:22.780
YUFENG GUO: Exactly.

00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:24.280
That we can tell
them apart, that we

00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:26.630
can distinguish between
instrument one, two, three,

00:13:26.630 --> 00:13:27.385
four, and five.

00:13:27.385 --> 00:13:28.260
NATALIE PIUCCO: Cool.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:28.760
OK.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:30.960
So we've got our training data.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:33.200
Now it's time to start
training the model.

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:35.360
YUFENG GUO: So
with AutoML, we can

00:13:35.360 --> 00:13:37.767
train models using
the data that we had,

00:13:37.767 --> 00:13:39.600
but then we don't have
to do any extra work.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:42.265
So we can see here that
the data is coming in.

00:13:42.265 --> 00:13:44.390
And you can see those are
some of the spectrograms.

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:47.210
Those are the real data that
attendees have generated

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:48.327
over the course of today.

00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:50.660
NATALIE PIUCCO: So they're
the images of the soundwaves.

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:51.170
YUFENG GUO: That's right.

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:52.295
These are the spectrograms.

00:13:52.295 --> 00:13:55.220
And then when you train a model,
it just does the training.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:57.560
There's not much kind
of work you have to do.

00:13:57.560 --> 00:13:59.030
And when it finishes
training, you

00:13:59.030 --> 00:14:00.890
can have some
evaluation data that

00:14:00.890 --> 00:14:04.130
shows you the metrics around
how well the model trained

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:04.965
and how it did.

00:14:04.965 --> 00:14:05.882
NATALIE PIUCCO: Right.

00:14:05.882 --> 00:14:08.522
So if you're new to ML,
that's a great space

00:14:08.522 --> 00:14:09.480
to get started, AutoML.

00:14:09.480 --> 00:14:10.522
YUFENG GUO: That's right.

00:14:10.522 --> 00:14:12.373
It's an easy tool
to get going with.

00:14:12.373 --> 00:14:13.790
And if you have
some data that you

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:17.090
want to throw at AutoML Vision,
it'll take care of it for you.

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.510
And you can go do something
else with your time.

00:14:19.510 --> 00:14:21.470
So we have this
board here, where

00:14:21.470 --> 00:14:23.360
you can move these
kind of sliders

00:14:23.360 --> 00:14:25.010
around to different positions.

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:28.370
And they map to different
values on the screen, which

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:32.240
change the values
of hyperparameters

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.250
of the actual models, things
like kernel width and height,

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:39.350
the layer size, the
number of strides,

00:14:39.350 --> 00:14:42.140
as well as the kind of
number of layers you have.

00:14:42.140 --> 00:14:44.600
And that will actually
change the amount of code

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:45.422
on the screen.

00:14:45.422 --> 00:14:47.630
Once we're happy with-- you
know, instead of settings

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:51.170
that we have, we can go ahead
and push the button here

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:52.700
and start a training job.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:55.850
And doing that, it
will assign us a name,

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:58.370
something fun like spinto-cap.

00:14:58.370 --> 00:15:01.610
And shoot that off
to Kubeflow, where we

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:03.020
have a pipeline to do training.

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:04.395
And when it finishes
training, it

00:15:04.395 --> 00:15:07.390
will deploy it on the Cloud AI
Platform Prediction Service.

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:08.390
NATALIE PIUCCO: Awesome.

00:15:08.390 --> 00:15:10.618
So time to move to stage
3 of training our model.

00:15:10.618 --> 00:15:11.660
YUFENG GUO: That's right.

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:12.887
Speaking of prediction.

00:15:12.887 --> 00:15:13.970
NATALIE PIUCCO: All right.

00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:16.960
So we're at the final stage now.

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:18.903
And that's to predict the notes.

00:15:18.903 --> 00:15:19.570
YUFENG GUO: Yes.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:21.640
And so we're going to
try to pull up the model

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:23.770
that we've been
training, spinto-cap.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:24.550
It's there for us.

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.950
So you can turn the
crank as a selector

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:28.750
and then press the red
button to select it.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:29.510
NATALIE PIUCCO: Select.

00:15:29.510 --> 00:15:32.220
YUFENG GUO: So this is kind of
like a customizable music box.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:35.800
And we can choose which notes
to play on which instrument.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.150
NATALIE PIUCCO: More xylophone.

00:15:37.150 --> 00:15:38.858
YUFENG GUO: You can
hit the button again.

00:15:38.858 --> 00:15:42.830
So in this countdown, then we'll
be able to turn this crank now.

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:44.680
And so these are the
notes that we set up.

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:48.780
And we're going to send those
notes off to the Cloud AI

00:15:48.780 --> 00:15:50.620
Platform Prediction Service.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:52.120
Now, how did we do, right?

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:53.480
Was our model any good?

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:56.370
We chose those parameters
kind of randomly.

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.180
And so it might be good.

00:15:58.180 --> 00:15:59.800
It might not be so good.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:00.600
We'll see, right?

00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:01.820
It got some of them.

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:04.028
It seemed like it might have
actually missed a bunch.

00:16:04.028 --> 00:16:04.530
It did.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:08.710
And so this is part of the
machine learning workflow.

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:10.960
This particular case, a
pretty abysmal outcome.

00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:11.160
NATALIE PIUCCO: Yeah.

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:12.220
But that's all
right, because that's

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.840
all part of machine
learning, right?

00:16:13.840 --> 00:16:14.125
YUFENG GUO: That's right.

00:16:14.125 --> 00:16:14.410
That's right.

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:16.285
NATALIE PIUCCO: It's
all part of the process.

00:16:16.285 --> 00:16:18.640
So I think this is a perfect
demonstration to show that.

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:22.243
So Yufeng, thank you so
much for this demonstration.

00:16:22.243 --> 00:16:24.160
I think it was very clear
in kind of the three

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.643
stages of what to expect
when I'm training machine

00:16:27.643 --> 00:16:28.310
learning models.

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:29.530
So thank you so much.

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:30.530
YUFENG GUO: My pleasure.

00:16:30.530 --> 00:16:31.840
Thanks so much, Natalie.

