00:20:37.963 --> 00:20:39.972
that they're essentially
doing their job well?

00:20:39.972 --> 00:20:41.930
But there are a lot,
because this is a software

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:43.560
stack with a lot of--

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:45.890
we've talked a lot about
decoupling and separation

00:20:45.890 --> 00:20:46.988
of concerns.

00:20:46.988 --> 00:20:48.530
You can have a
unified stack, but you

00:20:48.530 --> 00:20:51.072
have to recognize that there's
lots of folks that essentially

00:20:51.072 --> 00:20:52.700
are consumers of that stack.

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:53.900
STEPHANIE WONG: So what's
your advice for those

00:20:53.900 --> 00:20:55.858
who want to move to the
cloud with acceleration

00:20:55.858 --> 00:20:58.370
and not have to do a rip
and replace and manage

00:20:58.370 --> 00:20:59.810
their existing lifecycle?

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:01.418
How do they bring
in Cloud Native?

00:21:01.418 --> 00:21:02.210
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:04.370
I think Cloud Native
has really been

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:07.520
about sort of openness
and the flexibility

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:09.890
that we talked about today
with Anthos is really

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:13.310
there are an architectural set
of principles, which I think

00:21:13.310 --> 00:21:16.212
Kubernetes and things like
Istio have kind of put out there

00:21:16.212 --> 00:21:17.420
in the open source community.

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:19.830
There's a framework, and then
there's an implementation.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.920
So number one, I think
the industry is really

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:24.620
rallying around the fact
that container orchestration,

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:28.347
there's sort of a clear
de facto standard there.

00:21:28.347 --> 00:21:29.930
Obviously, we have
our managed version

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:32.222
of that, because there's a
lot beyond just the software

00:21:32.222 --> 00:21:33.890
bits in terms of
how do you actually

00:21:33.890 --> 00:21:37.340
do lifecycle management and keep
essentially automatic upgrades,

00:21:37.340 --> 00:21:41.180
patching, security updates, et
cetera, as part of this system

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:43.190
and so that developers
can move quickly,

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:45.560
but you can keep essentially
the security and stability

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:46.220
of the system.

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:46.550
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:21:46.550 --> 00:21:48.440
And one big part
of the acceleration

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:51.052
is GKE On-Prem, which was
announced last August.

00:21:51.052 --> 00:21:52.760
And this is the first
time we've actually

00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:55.530
brought our technology into
the data center at this scale.

00:21:55.530 --> 00:21:57.108
So why now and why not sooner?

00:21:57.108 --> 00:21:57.900
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah.

00:21:57.900 --> 00:21:59.150
I mean, what we
found is everybody

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:01.430
understands the benefits of
cloud, but in many cases,

00:22:01.430 --> 00:22:03.238
there's technical and
nontechnical reasons

00:22:03.238 --> 00:22:05.780
why they can't move all their
workloads into cloud overnight.

00:22:05.780 --> 00:22:08.847
So this was a lot about bringing
the best of the Kubernetes

00:22:08.847 --> 00:22:10.430
stack and what we're
doing with Google

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:12.830
Cloud to the on-prem
environment and helping

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:15.410
people move at their own
pace, while at the same time

00:22:15.410 --> 00:22:16.560
modernizing in place.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:18.500
So it's not just about
renting compute cycles,

00:22:18.500 --> 00:22:20.790
it's actually about
application modernization

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:22.408
and developing new
services and adding

00:22:22.408 --> 00:22:23.700
more business value, et cetera.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:25.380
So we're pretty
excited about that.

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:27.750
Now, it's not just
on our GCP resources,

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:30.840
but we can essentially run
this on third-party servers

00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:33.030
in a private data center
for the enterprise.

00:22:33.030 --> 00:22:35.550
And as we talked about today,
other environments as well.

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:36.580
STEPHANIE WONG: So
speaking of modernization,

00:22:36.580 --> 00:22:38.540
we loved your demo during
the day-one keynote.

00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:39.120
JENNIFER LIN: Thank you.

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:40.890
STEPHANIE WONG: We heard a
lot about modernizing in place

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:41.530
this year.

00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:43.410
Can you talk about
how Anthos enables

00:22:43.410 --> 00:22:44.950
you to modernize
your applications,

00:22:44.950 --> 00:22:46.742
no matter where they
are, as you mentioned,

00:22:46.742 --> 00:22:48.060
on-premise user and the cloud?

00:22:48.060 --> 00:22:48.180
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah.

00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:50.280
I think that's the nice
thing about Kubernetes

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:52.740
is with Anthos,
we're really thinking

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:55.440
about sort of open APIs
so that the interfaces

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:57.420
between those environments,
number one, we

00:22:57.420 --> 00:22:59.190
can hide the complexity
but still keep

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:01.770
a consistent management
environment for the developers.

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:03.270
Independent of where
those workloads

00:23:03.270 --> 00:23:06.420
run, they can learn
one set of tools

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:08.880
and essentially evolve
as the industry evolves

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:12.000
without picking sort of exactly
where that's going to run

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:15.012
or what production environment
it's going to run in.

00:23:15.012 --> 00:23:16.470
Because many
developers don't know.

00:23:16.470 --> 00:23:18.250
They want to write
once, run anywhere.

00:23:18.250 --> 00:23:20.730
Their consumers may be
running in many clouds.

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:23.670
And similarly for the
platform administrator,

00:23:23.670 --> 00:23:26.760
they don't have to learn a
bunch of different vendor tools.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:28.840
This framework is here to stay.

00:23:28.840 --> 00:23:31.560
We believe it's going to be
the standard for many years

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:32.260
to come.

00:23:32.260 --> 00:23:35.490
So even if that's not running
in Google's cloud per se,

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:37.320
that skill set that
many enterprises

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:40.140
are trying to hire people who
understand Kubernetes, that's

00:23:40.140 --> 00:23:40.800
very portable.

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:43.050
So people are willing
to invest in it now,

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:45.713
because it's going to be
here to stay for many years.

00:23:45.713 --> 00:23:47.130
STEPHANIE WONG:
And on that theme,

00:23:47.130 --> 00:23:49.130
we're hearing that
developers and operators want

00:23:49.130 --> 00:23:51.300
to be managing at higher
levels of the stack.

00:23:51.300 --> 00:23:54.030
But they still want visibility
and control over policy

00:23:54.030 --> 00:23:55.890
management at a service level.

00:23:55.890 --> 00:23:58.440
How is Anthos providing a
unified programming model

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:01.218
and monitoring and policy across
on-premise and multiple clouds?

00:24:01.218 --> 00:24:02.010
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah.

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:02.670
Great question.

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:04.200
I mean, with
Kubernetes, obviously,

00:24:04.200 --> 00:24:06.540
we've really thought about
container orchestration

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:09.060
and cluster
administration at scale.

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.992
With Istio, we're
thinking about--

00:24:10.992 --> 00:24:12.450
assuming most of
your microservices

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:13.825
are now containerized,
how do you

00:24:13.825 --> 00:24:16.230
look out for the lifecycle
and health of those services

00:24:16.230 --> 00:24:18.150
and the interactions
between those services?

00:24:18.150 --> 00:24:20.567
How do you make sure that those
services are authenticated

00:24:20.567 --> 00:24:24.360
and that you can put essentially
policies around those services

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:26.940
without having to manage the
underlying infrastructure

00:24:26.940 --> 00:24:27.743
complexity.

00:24:27.743 --> 00:24:29.160
And then we showed
how we're doing

00:24:29.160 --> 00:24:32.400
config management and automating
essentially configurations

00:24:32.400 --> 00:24:34.980
at scale in a way that
essentially is declarative

00:24:34.980 --> 00:24:37.317
so you can define the policies
once and push them down

00:24:37.317 --> 00:24:38.400
to different environments.

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:39.570
And you don't have
to rewrite them

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:41.640
with a lot of toil for
each cloud environment.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.560
And that's really
important for customers

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:45.435
that are thinking about,
let's say, you know,

00:24:45.435 --> 00:24:47.320
PCI compliance or governance.

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:49.620
Those rules around how
to have those security

00:24:49.620 --> 00:24:51.842
controls in place don't
change cloud to cloud.

00:24:51.842 --> 00:24:53.550
But today, they're
spending a lot of time

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:56.620
just to make sure they can
ensure audit and compliance

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:57.870
in each different environment.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:24:59.880
So we believe that's
sort of extra overhead

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:01.505
that doesn't need to
be there, and that

00:25:01.505 --> 00:25:03.270
was a key push with Anthos.

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:05.010
Many of our
Enterprise customers,

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:07.590
they want to embrace
new technologies.

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:09.180
But it's not easy
to figure out, how

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:11.590
do I ensure that I'm
still reducing my costs,

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:14.250
keeping the efficiency, and
addressing what the auditors

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:16.583
and compliance, regulatory
environments are looking for?

00:25:16.583 --> 00:25:17.500
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:19.440
And we actually know
that not everybody

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:21.060
has the luxury of
moving to the cloud

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:22.980
immediately for whatever reason.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:24.720
How can we help them get there?

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:24.870
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah.

00:25:24.870 --> 00:25:25.830
I think a lot of it is--

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:27.580
I mean, this has been
a great week so far.

00:25:27.580 --> 00:25:30.330
I think the partner
community is very engaged.

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:32.970
I think a lot of the use cases
and the best practices that

00:25:32.970 --> 00:25:35.970
are being put out there
are based on folks

00:25:35.970 --> 00:25:39.190
trying things out,
sharing best practices.

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:42.080
We're trying to take more of
a use-case-driven approach.

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:44.620
With Anthos, I think a lot
of what we're trying to share

00:25:44.620 --> 00:25:46.620
is the operational domain
knowledge and the best

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:49.810
practices of us having run
these systems at scale,

00:25:49.810 --> 00:25:53.370
but also tapping into our
hundreds of ecosystem partners

00:25:53.370 --> 00:25:55.830
who are getting excited about
it and contributing back.

00:25:55.830 --> 00:25:57.810
As this matures,
and I think this

00:25:57.810 --> 00:26:01.170
is happening very quickly
in the last couple of years,

00:26:01.170 --> 00:26:03.360
but it really is about
solution delivery and not

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:04.750
just technology delivery.

00:26:04.750 --> 00:26:06.750
STEPHANIE WONG: With
Anthos, all these customers

00:26:06.750 --> 00:26:09.697
moving into production at
scale is very exciting.

00:26:09.697 --> 00:26:12.030
And obviously, as you said,
there are security concerns.

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:14.620
How are we making that
easier for our customers?

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:15.412
JENNIFER LIN: Yeah.

00:26:15.412 --> 00:26:18.600
I mean, security is a major
differentiator for us for GCP

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:19.620
more broadly.

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:21.120
And I think because
containerization

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:23.580
is relatively a new
space, we've been doing

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:26.310
a lot with both our
customers and partners to do,

00:26:26.310 --> 00:26:29.130
number one, education and
awareness around container

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:31.440
security and secure
software supply chains

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:33.908
and things like binary
authorization and CI/CD

00:26:33.908 --> 00:26:35.700
and how to make sure
that security is baked

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:38.610
in way upstream so that when
a developer checks in code,

00:26:38.610 --> 00:26:42.150
essentially it starts the
governance process of security,

00:26:42.150 --> 00:26:45.340
and it's not a bolt-on
afterthought, after the fact.

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:48.060
I think that is a lot of the way
that the Google developers are.

00:26:48.060 --> 00:26:49.560
When they check in
code, essentially they

00:26:49.560 --> 00:26:51.185
don't have to be an
expert in security,

00:26:51.185 --> 00:26:53.640
but the platform takes care
of a lot of the complexity

00:26:53.640 --> 00:26:55.320
of security and governance.

00:26:55.320 --> 00:26:56.700
STEPHANIE WONG: Thank you so
much for your time, Jennifer.

00:26:56.700 --> 00:26:58.158
JENNIFER LIN: Thanks
for having me.

00:26:58.158 --> 00:27:00.840
Got a question or comment
on Anthos or Kubernetes,

00:27:00.840 --> 00:27:04.373
reach out to us on social media
with the hashtag #GoogleNext19.

00:27:04.373 --> 00:27:06.540
We're going to stick with
the Anthos topic for a bit

00:27:06.540 --> 00:27:07.998
and head down to
the Showcase floor

00:27:07.998 --> 00:27:10.800
where our team is demoing a new
solution that allows customers

00:27:10.800 --> 00:27:12.646
to modernize to containers.

00:27:29.727 --> 00:27:31.060
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Hey, everyone.

00:27:31.060 --> 00:27:32.430
We're here at the
Infrastructure and Migration

00:27:32.430 --> 00:27:33.830
Zone of Google Cloud Next.

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:34.770
I'm Mark Mirchandani.

00:27:34.770 --> 00:27:36.085
I'm here with Srinath.

00:27:36.085 --> 00:27:39.360
Srinath, we heard a
lot today about Anthos.

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:40.350
What is Anthos?

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:41.705
Why is it so exciting?

00:27:41.705 --> 00:27:44.080
SRINATH PADMANABHAN: Anthos
is incredibly exciting for us

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:45.180
at Google Cloud.

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:48.060
We announced Anthos as
Cloud Services Platform

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:49.730
just over eight months ago.

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:51.480
And today, we are very
excited to announce

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:54.390
general availability of
Anthos for our customers

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:55.700
to be able to use it.

00:27:55.700 --> 00:27:58.620
Anthos is our hybrid
and multi-cloud platform

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:00.360
that gives our
customers the ability

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:02.960
to build once and run anywhere.

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:05.460
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Why is hybrid
and multi-cloud so important

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:06.300
right now?

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:07.610
SRINATH PADMANABHAN: That's
a great question, Mark.

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:09.840
So hybrid and multi-cloud
has been on top of mind

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:11.310
for a lot of our customers.

00:28:11.310 --> 00:28:14.670
They're very, very excited
about the ability to use clouds.

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:17.490
But oftentimes what happens is
when they're running something

00:28:17.490 --> 00:28:19.890
on-prem but running
something else in the cloud,

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:21.450
it becomes hard
for them to manage

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:23.580
these different
deployments that make

00:28:23.580 --> 00:28:26.880
them segment their workforce
into engineers who are working

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:29.190
on different things instead
of focusing on innovating

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:30.520
for their customers.

00:28:30.520 --> 00:28:33.060
So that's why hybrid and
multi-cloud is very important,

00:28:33.060 --> 00:28:36.150
and it's very important to have
a uniform platform like Anthos

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:39.018
that lets customers run in a
uniform way across the board.

00:28:39.018 --> 00:28:41.310
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So we're
talking about standardizing,

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:43.683
making sure that everyone's
on the same level.

00:28:43.683 --> 00:28:45.350
For people who are
here at the showcase,

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:46.860
they can come interact
with this booth.

00:28:46.860 --> 00:28:48.960
What does this tell people
who are looking at what

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:50.310
does it Anthos do for me?

00:28:50.310 --> 00:28:52.352
SRINATH PADMANABHAN: So
what we have in this demo

00:28:52.352 --> 00:28:54.625
is we've taken the analogy
of a city and how it works,

00:28:54.625 --> 00:28:57.000
and we've used that to show
how hybrid and multi-cloud is

00:28:57.000 --> 00:28:59.130
very important and
how Anthos helps

00:28:59.130 --> 00:29:00.840
in that particular analogy.

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:03.340
So let's get started.

00:29:03.340 --> 00:29:05.460
So we have these three
things that you can do over

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:06.540
the course of this demo.

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:09.030
First, let's start out by
modernizing your services.

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:10.530
There are multiple
reasons you would

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:12.150
want to modernize your service.

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:15.600
For instance, it gives you
a lot more agility and speed

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:17.440
and lets you deploy faster.

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:19.310
So in this demo,
there are two options

00:29:19.310 --> 00:29:21.210
for how you can modernize
your applications.

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:23.740
You can either
containerize them manually

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:26.040
or you can use
CSP Migrate, which

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:27.990
is now called Anthos
Migrate, to be

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:30.750
able to migrate these
applications automatically

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:34.170
from VMs running on-prem
into containers running

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:34.890
in the cloud.

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:36.390
So for the sake of
this run-through,

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:38.220
let's select Modernizing
With Velostrata,

00:29:38.220 --> 00:29:42.210
which is Anthos Migrate.

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:44.340
So Velostrata
automatically containerizes

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:46.830
your VM-based workloads
and will deploy them

00:29:46.830 --> 00:29:49.920
to GKE running in the cloud.

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:51.570
So just as easily
as that, you'll

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:55.520
have all of your VMs now moved
to the cloud as containers.

00:29:55.520 --> 00:29:57.300
And now let's look
at the second part.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.520
We also help you standardize
config management

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:01.510
using Anthos Config Management.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:03.660
What Anthos Config
Management lets you do

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:05.880
is it lets you take a
single configuration

00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:09.600
and deploy it across the board,
declaratively and uniformly,

00:30:09.600 --> 00:30:13.170
without you having to go and do
it once at a time per platform.

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:15.900
So no matter whether you're
running only on-prem,

00:30:15.900 --> 00:30:18.240
in Google Cloud, or maybe
even with other clouds,

00:30:18.240 --> 00:30:19.407
you can do this all at once.

00:30:19.407 --> 00:30:20.407
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Right.

00:30:20.407 --> 00:30:21.660
So now it's all standardized.

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:23.770
SRINATH PADMANABHAN: So
now it's all standardized.

00:30:23.770 --> 00:30:25.620
And so for the third
part of this demo,

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:27.690
there's something
very exciting for me,

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:29.320
which is a service mesh.

00:30:29.320 --> 00:30:31.500
A service mesh is
basically a mechanism

00:30:31.500 --> 00:30:34.812
that lets you look deep into the
traffic that's actually flowing

00:30:34.812 --> 00:30:37.020
between your applications,
whether they're on-premise

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:37.920
or in the cloud.

00:30:37.920 --> 00:30:39.420
In this case, what
we're going to do

00:30:39.420 --> 00:30:41.545
is we're going to use Anthos
to implement a service

00:30:41.545 --> 00:30:43.950
mesh between on-prem
world here and the two

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:45.420
cloud worlds that we have.

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:47.940
And by doing this, we're
able to select a service

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:50.350
and we're able to say that
let's upgrade the service.

00:30:50.350 --> 00:30:52.380
Now, when you
upgrade the service,

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:55.260
you pick a new version that you
want to upgrade to, but instead

00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:57.960
of upgrading everything at
once, what you're going to do

00:30:57.960 --> 00:31:00.510
is you're going to select a
small percentage of your VMs

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:03.030
or workloads and
upgrade them at once.

00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:05.730
So when you do this and you
upgrade these containers

00:31:05.730 --> 00:31:08.790
at that point in time, you
will upgrade just that 20%.

00:31:08.790 --> 00:31:11.310
So that you see
everything's running fine,

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:13.150
and you don't have
anything broken.

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:15.960
And once you are confident that
this service is working well,

00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:17.550
you can upgrade everything else.

00:31:17.550 --> 00:31:19.800
So this means now you're
able to roll out applications

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:22.740
with limited downtime to almost
0 downtime for your customers.

00:31:22.740 --> 00:31:24.240
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So you can see how

00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:26.640
Anthos uses open source
technologies but also

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:29.560
this idea of standardization to
really bring it to everybody.

00:31:29.560 --> 00:31:29.820
SRINATH PADMANABHAN: Yes.

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:31.278
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
What's an example

00:31:31.278 --> 00:31:33.880
of a customer or concrete use
case that Anthos has right now?

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:34.510
SRINATH PADMANABHAN:
Great question.

00:31:34.510 --> 00:31:35.885
So we have a lot
of customers who

00:31:35.885 --> 00:31:37.950
have been very
excited to use Anthos,

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:39.600
and they span
multiple verticals.

00:31:39.600 --> 00:31:43.410
Like for instance, HSBC from
retail and banking and Siemens,

00:31:43.410 --> 00:31:45.720
who's a giant when it
comes to manufacturing,

00:31:45.720 --> 00:31:48.810
are also looking at Anthos
as their platform of choice

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:52.180
for doing IoT and
their IoT platform.

00:31:52.180 --> 00:31:54.330
So there are multiple
customers across verticals

00:31:54.330 --> 00:31:56.010
like retail and
health care who are

00:31:56.010 --> 00:31:58.260
very excited about
this platform and love

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:00.627
that ability to standardize
and have a uniform platform.

00:32:00.627 --> 00:32:01.710
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Perfect.

00:32:01.710 --> 00:32:02.820
Well, thank you so much Srinath.

00:32:02.820 --> 00:32:04.890
I'm sure there's going to be
tons of great content here

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:07.265
about Anthos and lots of other
stuff at Google Cloud Next

00:32:07.265 --> 00:32:07.980
so stay tuned.

00:32:18.798 --> 00:32:19.840
RETO MEIER: Thanks, team.

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:21.280
Check out this
next Dev Bite where

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:24.640
we explore how human bias can
sometimes creep into our work

00:32:24.640 --> 00:32:28.180
without us realizing it.

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:29.860
JOHN BOHANNON: This
is Michelle Casbon.

00:32:29.860 --> 00:32:31.998
She's an engineer at Google
on the Kubeflow team.

00:32:31.998 --> 00:32:33.790
MICHELLE CASBON: And
this is John Bohannon.

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:36.343
He's the Director of Science
at Primer, an AI startup.

00:32:36.343 --> 00:32:39.010
JOHN BOHANNON: My team at Primer
built a machine learning system

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:41.530
that reads millions of
news articles and tells you

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:42.245
what it learns.

00:32:42.245 --> 00:32:43.870
MICHELLE CASBON: And
I worked with them

00:32:43.870 --> 00:32:45.700
to deploy this
system of Kubeflow

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:47.773
to make it portable
and scalable.

00:32:47.773 --> 00:32:49.690
JOHN BOHANNON: The goal
is to create a machine

00:32:49.690 --> 00:32:51.430
to help humans learn
about the world

00:32:51.430 --> 00:32:53.260
and overcome their biases.

00:32:53.260 --> 00:32:55.300
Humans are biased.

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:56.918
MICHELLE CASBON:
Consider this riddle.

00:32:56.918 --> 00:32:59.210
JOHN BOHANNON: A boy and his
father are in a car crash.

00:32:59.210 --> 00:33:00.975
The father is killed instantly.

00:33:00.975 --> 00:33:02.350
The boy is rushed
to the hospital

00:33:02.350 --> 00:33:04.183
and prepped for surgery
with one of the best

00:33:04.183 --> 00:33:05.980
doctors in the country.

00:33:05.980 --> 00:33:08.140
The doctor arrives, sees
the boy on the table,

00:33:08.140 --> 00:33:10.180
and says I can't
operate on this patient.

00:33:10.180 --> 00:33:11.350
He's my son.

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:13.318
MICHELLE CASBON:
Who's the doctor?

00:33:13.318 --> 00:33:14.860
JOHN BOHANNON: It's
a paradox, right?

00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:16.280
Father's dead.

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:17.705
So--

00:33:17.705 --> 00:33:19.330
MICHELLE CASBON: It's
the boy's mother.

00:33:19.330 --> 00:33:21.705
JOHN BOHANNON: For those
playing at home, don't feel bad.

00:33:21.705 --> 00:33:23.595
85% of people are
stumped by this question.

00:33:23.595 --> 00:33:25.720
It even worked on Michelle
when she first heard it.

00:33:25.720 --> 00:33:27.610
MICHELLE CASBON:
Bias is powerful.

00:33:27.610 --> 00:33:29.110
All humans have it.

00:33:29.110 --> 00:33:31.248
And we all have to work
against it constantly.

00:33:31.248 --> 00:33:33.040
JOHN BOHANNON: Every
time you meet someone,

00:33:33.040 --> 00:33:35.540
you can't help but
make assumptions.

00:33:35.540 --> 00:33:37.577
MICHELLE CASBON: But
you're probably biased.

00:33:37.577 --> 00:33:38.410
JOHN BOHANNON: Wait.

00:33:38.410 --> 00:33:39.850
That one's actually mine.

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:41.470
MICHELLE CASBON:
And that one's mine.

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:42.710
Oh.

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:44.523
JOHN BOHANNON: You're
totally biased.

00:33:44.523 --> 00:33:46.690
MICHELLE CASBON: Any system
built on human decisions

00:33:46.690 --> 00:33:49.030
will inherit human bias.

00:33:49.030 --> 00:33:50.710
JOHN BOHANNON:
Consider Wikipedia.

00:33:50.710 --> 00:33:53.350
It has grown into the world's
most important general-purpose

00:33:53.350 --> 00:33:54.340
information resource.

00:33:54.340 --> 00:33:56.260
But there is no one in charge.

00:33:56.260 --> 00:33:58.120
Information is added
to Wikipedia day

00:33:58.120 --> 00:34:01.653
after day by millions
of volunteers.

00:34:01.653 --> 00:34:03.820
It just boggles the mind
that such a thing can exist

00:34:03.820 --> 00:34:06.040
and that it actually works.

00:34:06.040 --> 00:34:08.949
Wikipedia aims to be the
summary of all human knowledge.

00:34:08.949 --> 00:34:12.485
But it has biases, just like
the humans who create it.

00:34:12.485 --> 00:34:14.860
MICHELLE CASBON: If you think
about all the new things we

00:34:14.860 --> 00:34:17.650
learn about the world each day,
keeping Wikipedia up to date

00:34:17.650 --> 00:34:19.905
seems impossible.

00:34:19.905 --> 00:34:22.030
It's not just that important
information is missing

00:34:22.030 --> 00:34:25.330
from most articles, but it's
that many important articles

00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:27.280
haven't been written at all.

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:29.860
JOHN BOHANNON: Which shouldn't
be that surprising, right?

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:31.777
MICHELLE CASBON: Well,
so there are about five

00:34:31.777 --> 00:34:33.010
million Wikipedia articles.

00:34:33.010 --> 00:34:34.987
JOHN BOHANNON: In
English alone, yep.

00:34:34.987 --> 00:34:37.570
MICHELLE CASBON: And there are
some thousands of news articles

00:34:37.570 --> 00:34:38.830
published every day.

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:41.530
JOHN BOHANNON: Hundreds of
thousands, just in English.

00:34:41.530 --> 00:34:43.760
MICHELLE CASBON: So you
just have to read all of it.

00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:45.760
You find the important
information that's in one

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:49.000
set and doesn't
exist in the other.

00:34:49.000 --> 00:34:49.750
OK.

00:34:49.750 --> 00:34:50.785
Yeah, that's hard.

00:34:50.785 --> 00:34:53.469
JOHN BOHANNON: Machine
learning to the rescue.

00:34:53.469 --> 00:34:56.030
I decided to start by
focusing on people.

00:34:56.030 --> 00:34:59.080
We want a system that just
reads all the news every day

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:01.180
and learns new information
about everyone,

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:02.980
not only people
described in Wikipedia,

00:35:02.980 --> 00:35:05.485
but anyone described
in the news.

00:35:05.485 --> 00:35:07.360
And if there's enough
significant information

00:35:07.360 --> 00:35:09.910
about someone in the news,
who's missing from Wikipedia,

00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:13.180
the system should just write you
the first draft of the article.

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:14.770
The goal is to
make it as easy as

00:35:14.770 --> 00:35:18.353
possible for the human editors
of Wikipedia to do their job.

00:35:18.353 --> 00:35:20.770
And the first step is to shine
a really bright light so we

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:22.622
can see where our bias is.

00:35:22.622 --> 00:35:23.830
MICHELLE CASBON: For example?

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:26.110
JOHN BOHANNON: Well, for
example, only about 15%

00:35:26.110 --> 00:35:29.110
of Wikipedia's biographical
articles are about women.

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:32.560
And that gender gap is
especially bad for scientists.

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:35.050
If you never hear about all
the amazing women of science

00:35:35.050 --> 00:35:37.780
and engineering, then
it reinforces your bias

00:35:37.780 --> 00:35:39.400
about those professions.

00:35:39.400 --> 00:35:40.480
MICHELLE CASBON: Exactly.

00:35:40.480 --> 00:35:42.610
And that's why I wanted to
work with Primer on this

00:35:42.610 --> 00:35:43.685
in the first place.

00:35:43.685 --> 00:35:44.560
JOHN BOHANNON: Right.

00:35:44.560 --> 00:35:46.300
And here we are just
a few weeks later,

00:35:46.300 --> 00:35:47.925
and we're actually
catching up on where

00:35:47.925 --> 00:35:49.210
we are with deployment.

00:35:49.210 --> 00:35:53.380
So where we left off, we had
this really cool ML system

00:35:53.380 --> 00:35:56.300
with some really classic
ML deployment problems.

00:35:56.300 --> 00:35:58.285
MICHELLE CASBON:
Kubeflow to the rescue.

00:35:58.285 --> 00:36:00.160
JOHN BOHANNON: Our first
problem was scaling.

00:36:00.160 --> 00:36:02.830
So the computing load is all
over the map on this one.

00:36:02.830 --> 00:36:04.660
On any given day,
we're like processing

00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:06.580
hundreds of thousands
of documents-- news

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:08.950
articles, scientific
papers, and we're

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:12.050
updating information about
people in our knowledge base.

00:36:12.050 --> 00:36:14.740
But we also have users who
might want to add thousands

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:16.490
of new people all at once.

00:36:16.490 --> 00:36:19.000
And that kicks off a
backfill of information

00:36:19.000 --> 00:36:21.592
about those people from millions
of historical documents.

00:36:21.592 --> 00:36:23.800
MICHELLE CASBON: This turned
out to be easy to solve,

00:36:23.800 --> 00:36:27.730
because your team had already
set up a Kubernetes cluster.

00:36:27.730 --> 00:36:29.500
Now, Kubeflow is
built on Kubernetes,

00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:32.853
because scalability is
baked into the platform,

00:36:32.853 --> 00:36:34.270
though the application
backend was

00:36:34.270 --> 00:36:36.478
built on top of Postgres,
which was a straightforward

00:36:36.478 --> 00:36:38.330
migration into Cloud SQL.

00:36:38.330 --> 00:36:41.110
Once the data was migrated,
I transplanted the full app

00:36:41.110 --> 00:36:42.790
into Kubernetes Engine.

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:44.530
I started with a
single node cluster

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:46.900
and enabled
auto-provisioning, which

00:36:46.900 --> 00:36:49.210
scales the size of the
cluster up and down based

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:51.350
on resource requests.

00:36:51.350 --> 00:36:53.440
So now we can deal with
unpredictable and bursting

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:54.850
computing loads like yours.

00:36:54.850 --> 00:36:55.600
JOHN BOHANNON: OK.

00:36:55.600 --> 00:36:57.017
But on top of the
scaling problem,

00:36:57.017 --> 00:36:59.480
I have also got a
complexity problem.

00:36:59.480 --> 00:37:01.900
So our data pipeline has a
bunch of spaghetti junctions.

00:37:01.900 --> 00:37:04.840
And not only do
we have thousands

00:37:04.840 --> 00:37:06.530
of models to load
at inference time,

00:37:06.530 --> 00:37:08.140
but we have to
update those models

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:09.820
as new information comes in.

00:37:09.820 --> 00:37:12.130
So at training,
it adds up to like

00:37:12.130 --> 00:37:14.877
millions of model features
getting updated every day.

00:37:14.877 --> 00:37:16.210
MICHELLE CASBON: I noticed that.

00:37:16.210 --> 00:37:17.860
Luckily, this is
exactly what Kubeflow

00:37:17.860 --> 00:37:21.010
is designed for, composability.

00:37:21.010 --> 00:37:24.190
The platform provides patterns
for reusing common ML tasks

00:37:24.190 --> 00:37:25.990
like updating
models incrementally

00:37:25.990 --> 00:37:27.840
in the background.

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:29.920
I started by carving
out pieces of the app.

00:37:29.920 --> 00:37:31.870
For example, model
training was refactored

00:37:31.870 --> 00:37:36.370
into a separate service that
uses Kubeflow operators.

00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:40.150
The monolith is now separated
into smaller distinct pieces.

00:37:40.150 --> 00:37:43.660
Now it's a pipeline with three
separate steps-- train, serve,

00:37:43.660 --> 00:37:45.020
and webapp.

00:37:45.020 --> 00:37:46.770
JOHN BOHANNON: Oh,
that sounds much nicer.

00:37:46.770 --> 00:37:47.270
OK.

00:37:47.270 --> 00:37:48.537
So how's it looking so far?

00:37:48.537 --> 00:37:50.620
MICHELLE CASBON: Well, the
refactoring immediately

00:37:50.620 --> 00:37:51.970
reduced the training runtime.

00:37:51.970 --> 00:37:55.090
So that translates
directly to cost savings.

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:57.910
And now you don't have to
run huge nodes all the time,

00:37:57.910 --> 00:38:00.890
because GKE takes care
of the scaling for you.

00:38:00.890 --> 00:38:04.120
Well, the other one is the
decrease in code complexity.

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:07.630
That translates to less
maintenance overhead,

00:38:07.630 --> 00:38:10.810
less time searching for the
source of bugs, less time

00:38:10.810 --> 00:38:13.340
on ramping new engineers,
and adding new features.

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:14.090
JOHN BOHANNON: OK.

00:38:14.090 --> 00:38:15.478
But that creates a new problem.

00:38:15.478 --> 00:38:17.270
MICHELLE CASBON: What
haven't I thought of?

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:19.660
JOHN BOHANNON: So now we
have all this save time,

00:38:19.660 --> 00:38:21.285
what am I going to
do with all my time?

00:38:21.285 --> 00:38:23.660
MICHELLE CASBON: You could
create some Wikipedia articles

00:38:23.660 --> 00:38:24.700
about women of science.

00:38:24.700 --> 00:38:25.492
JOHN BOHANNON: Hmm.

00:38:25.492 --> 00:38:26.150
Good call.

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:27.983
Oh, and what about the
last thing on my wish

00:38:27.983 --> 00:38:29.810
list about vendor lock-in?

00:38:29.810 --> 00:38:32.600
We don't want our system tied
to one cloud infrastructure.

00:38:32.600 --> 00:38:36.110
We want it to be able to
deploy anywhere, even on-prem.

00:38:36.110 --> 00:38:38.000
Oh, and bonus points
if the solution

00:38:38.000 --> 00:38:40.230
is free and open source.

00:38:40.230 --> 00:38:41.660
MICHELLE CASBON:
Check and check.

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:43.577
As long as you're running
standard open source

00:38:43.577 --> 00:38:45.167
Kubernetes, you
can run Kubeflow.

00:38:45.167 --> 00:38:46.000
JOHN BOHANNON: Wait.

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:46.980
Kubeflow is open source?

00:38:46.980 --> 00:38:47.200
MICHELLE CASBON: Yep.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:48.517
JOHN BOHANNON: What?

00:38:48.517 --> 00:38:50.100
MICHELLE CASBON:
Thanks for listening.

00:38:50.100 --> 00:38:51.770
I hope that hearing
about this project

00:38:51.770 --> 00:38:54.080
lowers the bar for
trying it yourself.

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:56.150
The Kubeflow community
has some great resources

00:38:56.150 --> 00:38:59.237
for getting started, which
you can find kubeflow.org.

00:38:59.237 --> 00:39:01.570
JOHN BOHANNON: And, hey, got
a few minutes of free time?

00:39:01.570 --> 00:39:03.487
I've already used this
machine learning system

00:39:03.487 --> 00:39:05.660
to identify thousands of
notable women of science

00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:07.910
who are missing from Wikipedia.

00:39:07.910 --> 00:39:09.410
You can make a
difference right now.

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:11.930
Just follow this link
and see what the machine

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:13.490
has discovered for you.

00:39:13.490 --> 00:39:17.160
Go fill in a gap in
the world's knowledge.

00:39:17.160 --> 00:39:19.160
RETO MEIER: Eliminating
human bias with Kubeflow

00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:21.350
can be useful in every industry.

00:39:21.350 --> 00:39:24.230
You can watch all the livestream
content again or share it with

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:27.500
your friends and colleagues
at g.co/nextonair.

00:39:27.500 --> 00:39:29.510
Now, start working
up an appetite,

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:31.550
because right after
the next sessions,

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:34.280
we're experimenting
with pizza and pie.

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:34.930
Stay tuned.

00:39:34.930 --> 00:39:37.714
You're watching the Next
Live Show from San Francisco.

00:40:09.320 --> 00:40:10.070
Welcome, everyone.

00:40:10.070 --> 00:40:14.580
We are here at the DevZone at
