00:43:12.690 --> 00:43:14.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Whether it's pizza, pie,

00:43:14.440 --> 00:43:16.098
or anything else
round, you can't

00:43:16.098 --> 00:43:17.890
calculate the circumference
without knowing

00:43:17.890 --> 00:43:20.513
about 3.14, also known as pi.

00:43:20.513 --> 00:43:22.180
But there's a few
more digits than that.

00:43:22.180 --> 00:43:24.680
So let's talk to Emma and see
how she broke the world record

00:43:24.680 --> 00:43:27.890
for calculating 31.4 trillion
digits using Google Cloud

00:43:27.890 --> 00:43:29.320
Platform.

00:43:29.320 --> 00:43:31.260
So Emma, how did you do it?

00:43:31.260 --> 00:43:33.690
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: So we ran
a program called y-cruncher

00:43:33.690 --> 00:43:36.430
for four months on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:43:36.430 --> 00:43:39.340
And we set up a
cluster of 25 machines,

00:43:39.340 --> 00:43:43.330
and we made sure that these
machines ran continuously

00:43:43.330 --> 00:43:46.692
without any shutdowns
or reboots for 121 days.

00:43:46.692 --> 00:43:47.650
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:43:47.650 --> 00:43:49.140
I mean, that's no easy feat--

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:52.180
120 days, all those
machines running nonstop.

00:43:52.180 --> 00:43:53.980
That's kind of something
magical, right?

00:43:53.980 --> 00:43:54.190
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Yeah.

00:43:54.190 --> 00:43:56.890
But Google Cloud has all
the features and mechanisms

00:43:56.890 --> 00:44:00.770
to make sure these machines and
software are up and running.

00:44:00.770 --> 00:44:02.320
And we still have
[INAUDIBLE] systems

00:44:02.320 --> 00:44:06.757
and other techniques that we
use for our production software.

00:44:06.757 --> 00:44:08.590
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Because
normally speaking,

00:44:08.590 --> 00:44:11.890
it's very difficult to keep one
process running over 121 days,

00:44:11.890 --> 00:44:13.740
nonetheless on 24 machines.

00:44:13.740 --> 00:44:14.740
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Right.

00:44:14.740 --> 00:44:16.990
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So what
inspired you to kind of look

00:44:16.990 --> 00:44:18.630
for this many digits of pi?

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:20.680
EMMA HARUKA IWAO: Like,
humanity collectively

00:44:20.680 --> 00:44:23.230
tried to calculate
as many digits of pi

00:44:23.230 --> 00:44:26.290
as possible since like 2000 BC.

00:44:26.290 --> 00:44:29.740
And even the first
electronic computer, ENIAC,

00:44:29.740 --> 00:44:31.930
was used to calculate
2,000 digits of pi.

00:44:31.930 --> 00:44:35.260
And ever since then, pi
was used as a benchmark

00:44:35.260 --> 00:44:38.140
to measure the performance
and reliability of computers.

00:44:38.140 --> 00:44:39.910
And now, we use
the Google Cloud.

00:44:39.910 --> 00:44:42.730
So that means Cloud
is capable of doing

00:44:42.730 --> 00:44:44.068
this kind of heavy-duty work.

00:44:44.068 --> 00:44:45.860
MARK MIRCHANDANI: That's
awesome to see it.

00:44:45.860 --> 00:44:48.130
Congratulations on
breaking the world record.

00:44:48.130 --> 00:44:50.320
Of course, there's more
than just numbers in pi.

00:44:50.320 --> 00:44:52.240
We can also use it to make art.

00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:55.280
Let's talk to Mathias about
how he managed to do really,

00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:58.430
really cool generative art
using all of Emma's work.

00:44:58.430 --> 00:44:58.930
Mathias.

00:44:58.930 --> 00:44:59.680
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Hi.

00:44:59.680 --> 00:45:00.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Tell
me a little bit more

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:02.500
about what you've done here.

00:45:02.500 --> 00:45:03.583
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Right.

00:45:03.583 --> 00:45:06.240
So 31 trillion digits
is a huge number, right?

00:45:06.240 --> 00:45:06.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Absolutely.

00:45:06.640 --> 00:45:07.180
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN:
Right, but not

00:45:07.180 --> 00:45:08.750
only is it a lot
of numbers, what's

00:45:08.750 --> 00:45:10.310
really interesting
about pi, it's

00:45:10.310 --> 00:45:12.650
also that the sequence
never repeats itself.

00:45:12.650 --> 00:45:14.670
So we thought, like, what
could we do with this?

00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:17.300
So what we came up with is
we made this algorithm that

00:45:17.300 --> 00:45:20.780
would turn these sequences of
pi into a visual representation,

00:45:20.780 --> 00:45:21.530
essentially.

00:45:21.530 --> 00:45:24.140
And this allows people
to find 31 trillion

00:45:24.140 --> 00:45:28.328
different visual representations
of pi somewhere within pi.

00:45:28.328 --> 00:45:29.870
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Can we take a look?

00:45:29.870 --> 00:45:31.190
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Absolutely.

00:45:31.190 --> 00:45:33.830
So we can jump to any digit
within pi that we want.

00:45:33.830 --> 00:45:36.300
Now, just type in
a random number.

00:45:36.300 --> 00:45:40.320
So from that point on, it
takes the next several thousand

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:42.800
digits of pi, them being
unique within there,

00:45:42.800 --> 00:45:45.540
and then generate a piece of
art out of this, essentially.

00:45:45.540 --> 00:45:47.480
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So if anyone
else is here, they can come by,

00:45:47.480 --> 00:45:48.800
they can change up the numbers.

00:45:48.800 --> 00:45:50.420
And they'll see
different artwork,

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:52.087
because it's unique
sets of pi patterns.

00:45:52.087 --> 00:45:53.253
MATHIAS PAUMGARTEN: Exactly.

00:45:53.253 --> 00:45:55.420
So someone could come by
and say, like, they type

00:45:55.420 --> 00:45:57.600
in their phone number or
like maybe their birthday,

00:45:57.600 --> 00:46:00.020
the birthday of their
spouse or whatnot.

00:46:00.020 --> 00:46:01.970
And type this in
and then essentially

00:46:01.970 --> 00:46:04.903
get a unique representation
of that number within pi.

00:46:04.903 --> 00:46:06.320
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Very, very cool.

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:08.195
This is just one of the
many cool experiments

00:46:08.195 --> 00:46:09.800
that we have here
at the DevZone.

00:46:09.800 --> 00:46:11.200
So hopefully, the
people here can

00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:13.700
come check it out and see all
the cool work that we've done.

00:46:15.885 --> 00:46:17.510
STEPHANIE WONG: Thanks
very much, Mark.

00:46:17.510 --> 00:46:19.580
If you are hungry to
try these experiments,

00:46:19.580 --> 00:46:22.978
head to
g.co/showcase/experiments.

00:46:22.978 --> 00:46:25.520
Now, we don't have to tell you
how important mobile is to how

00:46:25.520 --> 00:46:27.380
we work, connect, and play.

00:46:27.380 --> 00:46:29.660
In this next Dev Bite,
we're going under the hood

00:46:29.660 --> 00:46:32.760
to show you how an ML kit can be
used for building mobile apps.

00:46:35.037 --> 00:46:37.370
IBRAHIM ULUKAYA: It seems
machine learning is everywhere

00:46:37.370 --> 00:46:38.480
nowadays.

00:46:38.480 --> 00:46:42.470
And it's so many offerings
for you as a mobile developer,

00:46:42.470 --> 00:46:46.650
it can get really confusing
as to where you should start.

00:46:46.650 --> 00:46:49.910
I'm Ibrahim Ulukaya, an
engineer on the Firebase team.

00:46:49.910 --> 00:46:51.830
And I'm here today
to introduce you

00:46:51.830 --> 00:46:54.830
to the options that
are available to you.

00:46:54.830 --> 00:46:57.480
If we could carry data
centers in our pocket

00:46:57.480 --> 00:47:00.650
with unlimited battery,
it would be much easier.

00:47:00.650 --> 00:47:03.410
But using ML for
mobile apps means

00:47:03.410 --> 00:47:07.340
thinking about the processing
power, battery consumption,

00:47:07.340 --> 00:47:10.740
and connectivity of your device.

00:47:10.740 --> 00:47:12.320
There are a number
of factors that

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:16.610
will dictate the ML strategy
and tools you'll want to use,

00:47:16.610 --> 00:47:19.340
like whether you want
to keep the data local,

00:47:19.340 --> 00:47:23.090
if you require low latency,
or if you need access

00:47:23.090 --> 00:47:26.240
to the processing
power of the cloud.

00:47:26.240 --> 00:47:29.570
Luckily, Google offers
a number of ML options

00:47:29.570 --> 00:47:32.030
for mobile developers
that can help

00:47:32.030 --> 00:47:36.830
you develop and deploy ML
models for the environment that

00:47:36.830 --> 00:47:39.110
fits your use case.

00:47:39.110 --> 00:47:43.310
To help you pick which ML
deployment option fits best,

00:47:43.310 --> 00:47:47.270
I'll walk you through a
few key decision points.

00:47:47.270 --> 00:47:49.580
The first question you
should ask yourself

00:47:49.580 --> 00:47:54.560
is where you want to perform
ML inferences, in the cloud

00:47:54.560 --> 00:47:56.210
or on the device.

00:47:56.210 --> 00:47:58.370
You would use the
cloud if you're already

00:47:58.370 --> 00:48:03.170
using an existing cloud
service for model inferences.

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:05.960
Or you run inferences
infrequently

00:48:05.960 --> 00:48:10.250
and want to minimize impact
on the device battery life

00:48:10.250 --> 00:48:15.320
or need to support older
less performing devices

00:48:15.320 --> 00:48:18.610
or if you need high-accuracy
models that require

00:48:18.610 --> 00:48:21.320
the compute power of the cloud.

00:48:21.320 --> 00:48:23.180
On the other hand,
there are cases

00:48:23.180 --> 00:48:25.690
in which it might
be useful to do ML

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:28.680
inference on the mobile device.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:33.290
For example, if you want to
keep data local to the device,

00:48:33.290 --> 00:48:37.640
you need low latency such as
when processing multiple frames

00:48:37.640 --> 00:48:39.530
in quick succession.

00:48:39.530 --> 00:48:44.270
You want inferences to work
even when the device is offline.

00:48:44.270 --> 00:48:47.540
Or your users may be
in an area of the world

00:48:47.540 --> 00:48:50.240
where high-speed
wireless connectivity

00:48:50.240 --> 00:48:53.520
is nonexistent or unreliable.

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:57.050
So now that you understand
all the constellations,

00:48:57.050 --> 00:49:00.690
let me present the
options available to you.

00:49:00.690 --> 00:49:04.110
First, is to run
inference in the cloud.

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:07.820
And there is a well of
tools and APIs you can use.

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:11.510
These include Cloud Vision
for image recognition

00:49:11.510 --> 00:49:14.720
and classification,
Cloud Natural Language

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:18.950
API for text parsing
and analysis,

00:49:18.950 --> 00:49:22.220
AutoML that allows you
to customize models

00:49:22.220 --> 00:49:24.800
that are sought from the cloud.

00:49:24.800 --> 00:49:28.010
All are available via REST APIs.

00:49:28.010 --> 00:49:30.830
I won't go into them all
here, but you can learn more

00:49:30.830 --> 00:49:34.600
at cloud.google.com
/products/ai/building.blocks.

00:49:39.470 --> 00:49:42.680
Next is to run
inference on the device.

00:49:42.680 --> 00:49:44.690
And you have two main options.

00:49:44.690 --> 00:49:47.600
You can either use
TF Lite directly

00:49:47.600 --> 00:49:50.560
or you can use a ML
Kit for Firebase.

00:49:50.560 --> 00:49:54.380
ML Kit is a mobile SDK that
brings Google's machine

00:49:54.380 --> 00:50:00.050
learning experience to Android
and iOS apps in a powerful, yet

00:50:00.050 --> 00:50:01.880
easy-to-use package.

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:04.670
It comes with a set
of ready-to-use APIs,

00:50:04.670 --> 00:50:08.240
both for on device and
cloud-based inference.

00:50:08.240 --> 00:50:10.270
They support common
mobile use cases

00:50:10.270 --> 00:50:15.760
like image labeling, face
detection, text recognition,

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:18.500
and barcode scanning,
to name a few.

00:50:18.500 --> 00:50:21.790
You simply pass in the
data to the ML Kit Library,

00:50:21.790 --> 00:50:24.500
and it will give you the
information you need.

00:50:24.500 --> 00:50:28.060
Whether you are new to or
experienced with machine

00:50:28.060 --> 00:50:30.400
learning, you can
implement the functionality

00:50:30.400 --> 00:50:33.350
you need all in a
few lines of code.

00:50:33.350 --> 00:50:37.020
And it includes both
Android and iOS libraries.

00:50:37.020 --> 00:50:41.380
While the on-device APIs
process data quickly and move it

00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:44.470
even when there is no
need for a connection.

00:50:44.470 --> 00:50:46.780
The cloud-based APIs
leverage the power

00:50:46.780 --> 00:50:49.300
of Google Cloud
Platform's machine

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:53.680
learning technology to give
a higher level of accuracy.

00:50:53.680 --> 00:50:57.540
If you have a use case that's
different than offered models,

00:50:57.540 --> 00:51:01.630
ML Kit also offers the ability
to deploy and experiment

00:51:01.630 --> 00:51:05.050
with your own custom
TensorFlow Lite models

00:51:05.050 --> 00:51:08.350
to run on device inference.

00:51:08.350 --> 00:51:10.170
It works like this--

00:51:10.170 --> 00:51:13.830
after converting your model
to the TF Lite format,

00:51:13.830 --> 00:51:17.600
you can host and serve
it to your users.

00:51:17.600 --> 00:51:21.720
ML Kit for Firebase can
then be used to host

00:51:21.720 --> 00:51:26.050
and dynamically serve TF
Lite models to your users.

00:51:26.050 --> 00:51:28.500
And it provides
convenient APIs that

00:51:28.500 --> 00:51:33.690
help you use your custom TF
Lite models in your mobile apps.

00:51:33.690 --> 00:51:36.420
With ML Kit for
Firebase, you can

00:51:36.420 --> 00:51:39.660
reduce the initial install
size of your mobile app

00:51:39.660 --> 00:51:42.660
and only download the
model that's needed.

00:51:42.660 --> 00:51:45.090
Dynamically swap
models on the fly

00:51:45.090 --> 00:51:48.840
without having to republish
your app to the App Store.

00:51:48.840 --> 00:51:52.410
Target different user
segments with models

00:51:52.410 --> 00:51:55.350
tailored specifically for them.

00:51:55.350 --> 00:51:58.650
Run AP experiments
with multiple models

00:51:58.650 --> 00:52:01.320
to find the best performing one.

00:52:01.320 --> 00:52:04.260
If you'd like, you can
also use your TF Lite

00:52:04.260 --> 00:52:08.550
model on mobile directly
with TF Lite APIs.

00:52:08.550 --> 00:52:12.010
These offer blazingly
fast inference times,

00:52:12.010 --> 00:52:16.320
particularly on Android devices
that support the neural network

00:52:16.320 --> 00:52:17.520
APIs.

00:52:17.520 --> 00:52:21.030
And the variety of ways to
host and deploy your model

00:52:21.030 --> 00:52:24.450
for mobile developers looking
to implement machine learning

00:52:24.450 --> 00:52:28.530
into their apps, there are
several directions to choose,

00:52:28.530 --> 00:52:31.980
depending on how much
flexibility you want

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:35.640
or how specific
your use case is.

00:52:35.640 --> 00:52:37.980
We covered some key
factors that help

00:52:37.980 --> 00:52:42.840
you decide, like whether you
do your inference on the device

00:52:42.840 --> 00:52:45.210
or in the cloud and
whether you want

00:52:45.210 --> 00:52:49.350
to use the pretrained
models Google provides

00:52:49.350 --> 00:52:52.230
or train and bring
your custom model.

00:52:52.230 --> 00:52:56.130
Check out our full session at
Next for a deep-dive analysis,

00:52:56.130 --> 00:52:59.910
live experiments, and the
latest ML offerings from Google.

