00:58:20.435 --> 00:58:21.060
RETO MEIER: OK.

00:58:21.060 --> 00:58:24.280
Let's get down to business and
get to the nitty gritty of SRE.

00:58:24.280 --> 00:58:26.730
How do we help customers
move towards implementing

00:58:26.730 --> 00:58:27.313
SRE practices?

00:58:27.313 --> 00:58:29.522
DAVE RENSIN: Well, so there
are two things you really

00:58:29.522 --> 00:58:30.360
have to do.

00:58:30.360 --> 00:58:32.360
One, you have to make it
easy for them to learn.

00:58:32.360 --> 00:58:32.650
Right?

00:58:32.650 --> 00:58:34.352
So we wrote these two
bestselling books.

00:58:34.352 --> 00:58:35.560
So we have a Coursera course.

00:58:35.560 --> 00:58:37.865
So we do a lot of writing
and public speaking.

00:58:37.865 --> 00:58:40.240
And then you have to make it
easy for them to practice it

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:41.090
in the tools.

00:58:41.090 --> 00:58:41.590
Right?

00:58:41.590 --> 00:58:43.870
So there is a wonderful
announcement today

00:58:43.870 --> 00:58:47.930
from the Stackdriver team about
SLO alerting and error budget

00:58:47.930 --> 00:58:48.430
monitoring.

00:58:48.430 --> 00:58:50.803
So those are really
the two ways you

00:58:50.803 --> 00:58:51.970
have to attack that problem.

00:58:51.970 --> 00:58:52.887
RETO MEIER: Fantastic.

00:58:52.887 --> 00:58:55.000
So what is Google doing
to help our customers meet

00:58:55.000 --> 00:58:55.710
their own SLOs?

00:58:55.710 --> 00:58:58.210
DAVE RENSIN: You know, I think
the best thing a customer can

00:58:58.210 --> 00:59:04.020
do to reliably meet SLOs,
besides building good products,

00:59:04.020 --> 00:59:05.980
is actually share their
monitoring with Google.

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:07.630
Like, if you're
using Stackdriver,

00:59:07.630 --> 00:59:12.100
you set up an SLO dashboard, you
can actually share that with us

00:59:12.100 --> 00:59:14.513
so that when you have difficult
moments, and you will,

00:59:14.513 --> 00:59:16.180
it becomes faster for
everyone to debug.

00:59:16.180 --> 00:59:17.700
RETO MEIER: Nice.

00:59:17.700 --> 00:59:18.820
And so what is Google--

00:59:18.820 --> 00:59:20.710
what is Google doing
with our products

00:59:20.710 --> 00:59:22.720
to help customers implement
those SLOs and error

00:59:22.720 --> 00:59:23.680
budgets themselves?

00:59:23.680 --> 00:59:25.900
DAVE RENSIN: My
personal goal at Google

00:59:25.900 --> 00:59:29.320
is to make sure that it's
easy in every product

00:59:29.320 --> 00:59:32.887
to practice SRE principles, that
rollouts and rollbacks are item

00:59:32.887 --> 00:59:35.220
potent and easy when you're
doing them on, I don't know,

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:37.138
GCE or GKE.

00:59:37.138 --> 00:59:38.680
You saw the announcement
this morning

00:59:38.680 --> 00:59:42.100
of our new Kubernetes
hybrid and on-prem solution.

00:59:42.100 --> 00:59:44.350
We're going to make some
other announcements about SLO

00:59:44.350 --> 00:59:47.022
monitoring and burn monitoring
in those solutions too.

00:59:47.022 --> 00:59:49.230
So we're really building at
every layer of the stack.

00:59:49.230 --> 00:59:50.200
RETO MEIER: Fantastic.

00:59:50.200 --> 00:59:52.640
Now, I've been hanging
out with a few SREs.

00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:55.575
I hear a lot about
accepting failure as normal.

00:59:55.575 --> 00:59:56.950
How do you recommend
customers go

00:59:56.950 --> 01:00:00.220
about moving from every area
is a problem to the mindset

01:00:00.220 --> 01:00:01.270
of failure is normal?

01:00:01.270 --> 01:00:02.770
DAVE RENSIN: You
know, a big problem

01:00:02.770 --> 01:00:04.270
is for so long we've
all been living

01:00:04.270 --> 01:00:06.180
under the tyranny of perfection.

01:00:06.180 --> 01:00:07.530
We expect perfection.

01:00:07.530 --> 01:00:09.280
And I would say to
customers, tell me

01:00:09.280 --> 01:00:12.850
one system designed by
humans, software or hardware,

01:00:12.850 --> 01:00:14.840
economic, political,
anything you want,

01:00:14.840 --> 01:00:16.860
that's ever been 100% reliable.

01:00:16.860 --> 01:00:18.710
And they can't, and you
know why they can't?

01:00:18.710 --> 01:00:21.280
Because nature has
never built one either.

01:00:21.280 --> 01:00:24.250
So once you accept that
it's not possible to do,

01:00:24.250 --> 01:00:27.550
then the question becomes, well,
how much failure is acceptable?

01:00:27.550 --> 01:00:29.080
Because you're
going to have some.

01:00:29.080 --> 01:00:30.760
And then we get into
the conversations

01:00:30.760 --> 01:00:32.740
about error budgets and SLOs.

01:00:32.740 --> 01:00:34.520
RETO MEIER: Yeah,
that's a good segue.

01:00:34.520 --> 01:00:36.220
So how do you see
customers becoming

01:00:36.220 --> 01:00:39.843
effective at managing the
workloads of their SRE teams?

01:00:39.843 --> 01:00:40.510
DAVE RENSIN: OK.

01:00:40.510 --> 01:00:43.990
So, you know, there are some
good rule of thumb limits

01:00:43.990 --> 01:00:46.180
about the number of
incidents you can really

01:00:46.180 --> 01:00:49.990
have during an on-call period
or how much of your project time

01:00:49.990 --> 01:00:50.630
you can have.

01:00:50.630 --> 01:00:52.480
So I think a good rule
of thumb for people

01:00:52.480 --> 01:00:56.110
is if you see your teams are
spending at all anything less

01:00:56.110 --> 01:00:59.890
than 50% of their calendar
time, their clock time,

01:00:59.890 --> 01:01:03.790
on engineering projects,
they're headed for toil.

01:01:03.790 --> 01:01:05.440
And they're not
managing the load well.

01:01:05.440 --> 01:01:07.360
But if you come talk
to us, we can help you

01:01:07.360 --> 01:01:08.230
manage your way through that.

01:01:08.230 --> 01:01:08.650
RETO MEIER: Absolutely.

01:01:08.650 --> 01:01:10.960
And so speaking of that,
what are your recommendations

