01:02:16.150 --> 01:02:19.482
and how to fix them
using Stackdriver.

01:02:19.482 --> 01:02:21.190
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: As
you move to the cloud

01:02:21.190 --> 01:02:24.010
and adopt the latest
technology such as Kubernetes,

01:02:24.010 --> 01:02:27.370
have you found it even harder
to find the root cause of issues

01:02:27.370 --> 01:02:30.970
and understand just how well
your application is performing?

01:02:30.970 --> 01:02:33.250
Stackdriver Application
Performance Management,

01:02:33.250 --> 01:02:36.880
AKA APM, shines light
on your cloud native

01:02:36.880 --> 01:02:38.680
and multi-cloud
environments in order

01:02:38.680 --> 01:02:40.990
to help you solve
these problems better.

01:02:40.990 --> 01:02:43.690
I'm Bryan Zimmerman, Product
Manager for Trace and Debugger.

01:02:43.690 --> 01:02:45.898
MORGAN MCLEAN: And I'm Morgan
McLean, Product Manager

01:02:45.898 --> 01:02:47.208
for Stackdriver Profiler.

01:02:47.208 --> 01:02:49.750
Stackdriver is Google's suite
of management and observability

01:02:49.750 --> 01:02:50.410
products.

01:02:50.410 --> 01:02:52.720
APM is focused on
that deeper levels

01:02:52.720 --> 01:02:54.340
of debug-style
information to help

01:02:54.340 --> 01:02:57.700
produce mean time to resolution,
find performance optimizations,

01:02:57.700 --> 01:03:00.400
and build the best possible
experience for your users.

01:03:00.400 --> 01:03:01.960
APM features three products--

01:03:01.960 --> 01:03:04.120
Stackdriver Trace,
Stackdriver Profiler,

01:03:04.120 --> 01:03:05.442
and Stackdriver Debugger.

01:03:05.442 --> 01:03:07.900
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: Trace is our
distributing tracing product.

01:03:07.900 --> 01:03:09.900
What is distributed
tracing, you ask?

01:03:09.900 --> 01:03:13.000
Well, tracing allows you to
visualize how a request flows

01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:14.610
through your environment.

01:03:14.610 --> 01:03:16.870
In microservices applications
or highly distributed

01:03:16.870 --> 01:03:20.110
environments, understanding
context and flow

01:03:20.110 --> 01:03:22.332
is essential to
resolving problems.

01:03:22.332 --> 01:03:24.290
MORGAN MCLEAN: How do we
use this in real life?

01:03:24.290 --> 01:03:25.665
For example, let's
assume that we

01:03:25.665 --> 01:03:27.580
receive an alert that
shows a jump in latency

01:03:27.580 --> 01:03:28.870
for our application.

01:03:28.870 --> 01:03:30.730
Trace gives us the
best detailed data

01:03:30.730 --> 01:03:32.273
to help us troubleshoot
this error.

01:03:32.273 --> 01:03:33.940
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: With
Stackdriver Trace,

01:03:33.940 --> 01:03:36.310
you can easily see
the top requests,

01:03:36.310 --> 01:03:39.610
RPC calls and
associated latency,

01:03:39.610 --> 01:03:41.140
find an example of
the issue you're

01:03:41.140 --> 01:03:44.710
having with simple
search and filter,

01:03:44.710 --> 01:03:46.510
and see a simple
view of the request

01:03:46.510 --> 01:03:50.090
as it processes through
your complex environment.

01:03:50.090 --> 01:03:53.185
This includes detailed custom
information and annotations.

01:03:53.185 --> 01:03:55.060
MORGAN MCLEAN: For more
detailed information,

01:03:55.060 --> 01:03:57.477
analysis reports are
generated automatically.

01:03:57.477 --> 01:03:59.560
You can view an existing
report or create your own

01:03:59.560 --> 01:04:01.970
to see how latency
has changed over time.

01:04:01.970 --> 01:04:03.550
You can group these
by percentile,

01:04:03.550 --> 01:04:05.740
and you can also drill
down into example traces

01:04:05.740 --> 01:04:08.527
to find out exactly what
happened to cause this issue.

01:04:08.527 --> 01:04:11.110
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: As you can see,
Trace guides you from problem

01:04:11.110 --> 01:04:12.340
to service.

01:04:12.340 --> 01:04:15.070
Now we will show you how you
can get from service to method

01:04:15.070 --> 01:04:16.180
with Stackdriver Profiler.

01:04:16.180 --> 01:04:18.430
MORGAN MCLEAN: Profiler can
inspect the CPU and memory

01:04:18.430 --> 01:04:20.500
performance of all the
functions in your code.

01:04:20.500 --> 01:04:22.930
And it shows you the calling
relationships between them.

01:04:22.930 --> 01:04:25.630
Profiler can visualize your
code on a flame graph, which

01:04:25.630 --> 01:04:28.120
shows calling relationships
along the vertical axis

01:04:28.120 --> 01:04:30.420
and resource consumption
on the horizontal axis.

01:04:30.420 --> 01:04:32.170
You can use a number
of different filters,

01:04:32.170 --> 01:04:33.610
including weight
filtering, which

01:04:33.610 --> 01:04:36.700
only shows data captured during
periods of high consumption,

01:04:36.700 --> 01:04:39.130
and a focus filter, which
shows all the pass in

01:04:39.130 --> 01:04:41.153
and out of a selected function.

01:04:41.153 --> 01:04:43.570
If you need to visualize the
performance of functions that

01:04:43.570 --> 01:04:45.112
are called throughout
your code base,

01:04:45.112 --> 01:04:46.630
you can use the
Focus Table, which

01:04:46.630 --> 01:04:49.250
shows the aggregated costs of
all the functions in the graph.

01:04:49.250 --> 01:04:51.490
Finally, once you've
identified the area of code

01:04:51.490 --> 01:04:52.990
that you're interested
in, it's time

01:04:52.990 --> 01:04:55.508
to inspect the behavior of
this code to find the problem.

01:04:55.508 --> 01:04:57.550
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: This is
where Debugger comes in.

01:04:57.550 --> 01:04:59.410
Stackdriver Debugger
offers the ability

01:04:59.410 --> 01:05:02.770
to take log points and
snapshots of your running code

01:05:02.770 --> 01:05:06.460
in production, without impact,
redeploying, or restarting

01:05:06.460 --> 01:05:08.200
your services.

01:05:08.200 --> 01:05:10.180
Traditionally,
debugging involves

01:05:10.180 --> 01:05:14.140
analyzing logs and metrics,
taking a guess at the problem,

01:05:14.140 --> 01:05:16.750
deploying some new
code or a debug module,

01:05:16.750 --> 01:05:20.560
looking at the logs again to
confirm, waiting for the issue,

01:05:20.560 --> 01:05:23.680
analyzing logs again, perhaps
taking another crack at it.

01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:25.540
Stackdriver Debugger
allows you to take

01:05:25.540 --> 01:05:28.240
breakpoints and
snapshots in production

01:05:28.240 --> 01:05:31.330
without system impact,
redeploying, or restarting

01:05:31.330 --> 01:05:32.223
your application.

01:05:32.223 --> 01:05:34.390
This allows you to tighten
your troubleshooting flow

01:05:34.390 --> 01:05:36.490
significantly, as you're
not having to wait

01:05:36.490 --> 01:05:38.820
for lengthy deployments.

01:05:38.820 --> 01:05:40.740
In addition to the
efficiency gains,

01:05:40.740 --> 01:05:44.100
many customers report being
able to bring debugging further

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:48.060
up the troubleshooting
stack to operators or SREs,

01:05:48.060 --> 01:05:51.608
further reducing MTTR and
affecting customer impact.

01:05:51.608 --> 01:05:54.150
MORGAN MCLEAN: All of these APM
tools work with code and apps

01:05:54.150 --> 01:05:56.940
that are running on any cloud or
even on-premise infrastructure.

01:05:56.940 --> 01:05:58.830
So no matter where you
run your application,

01:05:58.830 --> 01:06:01.400
you now have a consistent
accessible APM tool

01:06:01.400 --> 01:06:03.750
kit to monitor and manage
the performance of your apps.

01:06:03.750 --> 01:06:06.000
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: With
Stackdriver Trace, Profiler,

01:06:06.000 --> 01:06:09.180
and Debugger, APM enables
developers and operators

01:06:09.180 --> 01:06:11.520
to track and resolve issues
the way that Google Site

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:14.280
Reliability Engineering does.

01:06:14.280 --> 01:06:17.550
You can start and stop your code
without affecting your users

01:06:17.550 --> 01:06:19.650
and get insights into
how your code is running

01:06:19.650 --> 01:06:21.692
on production, no matter
what cloud you're using.

01:06:21.692 --> 01:06:24.150
MORGAN MCLEAN: Whether your
application is just getting off

01:06:24.150 --> 01:06:26.670
the ground or if it's already
live and in production,

01:06:26.670 --> 01:06:28.830
using APM to monitor
and tune its performance

01:06:28.830 --> 01:06:30.270
can be a game changer.

01:06:30.270 --> 01:06:32.130
To get started with
Stackdriver APM,

01:06:32.130 --> 01:06:33.930
simply link the
appropriate libraries

01:06:33.930 --> 01:06:35.600
for each tool to
your application

01:06:35.600 --> 01:06:38.040
and then start gathering
telemetry for analysis.

01:06:38.040 --> 01:06:40.615
BRYAN ZIMMERMAN: To learn more,
visit cloud.google.com/apm.

