01:14:12.330 --> 01:14:13.538
RETO MEIER: That's fantastic.

01:14:13.538 --> 01:14:16.830
And what ML AI launches
are you most excited about

01:14:16.830 --> 01:14:18.288
and tell me a little
bit about why.

01:14:18.288 --> 01:14:20.080
SARA ROBINSON: There's
some AutoML launches

01:14:20.080 --> 01:14:22.290
that I'm super excited
about, really making

01:14:22.290 --> 01:14:23.820
it easy for anyone
to use machine

01:14:23.820 --> 01:14:26.600
learning without having to have
machine learning expertise.

01:14:26.600 --> 01:14:29.132
So the first I'll talk
about is AutoML Tables,

01:14:29.132 --> 01:14:31.590
which makes it really easy to
build custom machine learning

01:14:31.590 --> 01:14:32.673
models on structured data.

01:14:32.673 --> 01:14:34.048
So think about
anything you might

01:14:34.048 --> 01:14:35.580
be able to put in
a spreadsheet--

01:14:35.580 --> 01:14:38.700
categorical data, numerical
data-- building models on that.

01:14:38.700 --> 01:14:40.810
With AutoML Tables,
that's really easy.

01:14:40.810 --> 01:14:43.170
Upload your data to the
UI, press the Train button,

01:14:43.170 --> 01:14:45.105
and your model's ready to go.

01:14:45.105 --> 01:14:46.980
Another AutoML launch
I'm super excited about

01:14:46.980 --> 01:14:49.620
is AutoML Vision
Object Detection.

01:14:49.620 --> 01:14:52.235
So AutoML Vision can
already do classification,

01:14:52.235 --> 01:14:53.610
but now what you'll
be able to do

01:14:53.610 --> 01:14:55.740
is identify regions
in your image

01:14:55.740 --> 01:14:57.570
where a certain label exists.

01:14:57.570 --> 01:15:00.020
So it will return
bounding boxes.

01:15:00.020 --> 01:15:02.210
So I'm super excited about
those AutoML launches.

01:15:02.210 --> 01:15:03.320
RETO MEIER: Very cool.

01:15:03.320 --> 01:15:05.250
And we're about to see
a showcase highlighting

01:15:05.250 --> 01:15:08.280
our work with the NCAA
using BigQuery ML.

01:15:08.280 --> 01:15:11.010
What can you tell us about
BigQuery ML specifically?

01:15:11.010 --> 01:15:13.860
SARA ROBINSON: BQML let's
folks that have structured

01:15:13.860 --> 01:15:15.120
data stored in BigQuery--

01:15:15.120 --> 01:15:17.520
BigQuery is our cloud
data analytics warehouse--

01:15:17.520 --> 01:15:19.680
so it lets you create
machine learning models on

01:15:19.680 --> 01:15:22.630
that data stored in BigQuery,
just with a single SQL query.

01:15:22.630 --> 01:15:25.410
So you don't have to move your
data out of BigQuery to use it.

01:15:25.410 --> 01:15:26.630
It's super simple to use.

01:15:26.630 --> 01:15:27.900
So you just write a query.

01:15:27.900 --> 01:15:29.040
Your model is
trained, and then you

01:15:29.040 --> 01:15:31.082
can write another query
to generate a prediction.

01:15:31.082 --> 01:15:33.180
And I actually gave
a session on BQML

01:15:33.180 --> 01:15:35.340
earlier today with
one of our customers,

01:15:35.340 --> 01:15:38.340
AAA, that's using BQML
to predict call volumes

01:15:38.340 --> 01:15:39.830
across their call centers.

01:15:39.830 --> 01:15:40.747
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

01:15:40.747 --> 01:15:43.745
And Rachael, have you also heard
about BQML in the launch too?

01:15:43.745 --> 01:15:45.120
