ALOK PATTANI: Yeah.

01:19:59.940 --> 01:20:01.620
STEPHANIE WONG: Wow,
amazing Showcase.

01:20:01.620 --> 01:20:03.840
How is BigQuery ML
changing the game

01:20:03.840 --> 01:20:06.825
for machine learning for
developers and practitioners?

01:20:06.825 --> 01:20:08.430
RACHAEL TATMAN:
Yeah, good question.

01:20:08.430 --> 01:20:10.290
Two things that come
to mind for me--

01:20:10.290 --> 01:20:12.960
first of all, being
able to work in SQL.

01:20:12.960 --> 01:20:15.180
So something we found in
our Kaggle Machine Learning

01:20:15.180 --> 01:20:16.890
Developer Survey is
that SQL is actually

01:20:16.890 --> 01:20:19.452
the third most commonly used
language for machine learning.

01:20:19.452 --> 01:20:21.660
And most of the people who
are outside of the machine

01:20:21.660 --> 01:20:24.210
learning AI space or just
coming into it, many of them

01:20:24.210 --> 01:20:25.520
are already familiar with SQL.

01:20:25.520 --> 01:20:27.978
So being able to use tools that
you already know how to use

01:20:27.978 --> 01:20:29.487
is a really big time saver.

01:20:29.487 --> 01:20:31.570
And also, you don't have
to download data locally.

01:20:31.570 --> 01:20:34.170
So if you've got sensitive data
that you can't store locally

01:20:34.170 --> 01:20:36.780
or it's just like really big,
and you can't store it on disk,

01:20:36.780 --> 01:20:41.070
being able to do your regression
in BigQuery instead of having

01:20:41.070 --> 01:20:43.255
to download everything,
do your Python code,

01:20:43.255 --> 01:20:45.630
and then upload everything
once you've changed your model

01:20:45.630 --> 01:20:46.673
can be a big time saver.

01:20:46.673 --> 01:20:48.090
SARA ROBINSON: And
to add to that,

01:20:48.090 --> 01:20:50.465
I think BQML is a great entry
point for machine learning.

01:20:50.465 --> 01:20:52.632
So you don't have to worry
about any sort of feature

01:20:52.632 --> 01:20:53.132
engineering.

01:20:53.132 --> 01:20:55.007
So if you have categorical
data, for example,

01:20:55.007 --> 01:20:56.550
if you're building
a model yourself,

01:20:56.550 --> 01:20:59.240
you would have to one hot
encode that into arrays.

01:20:59.240 --> 01:21:01.740
BQML can recognize that
it's a categorical column

01:21:01.740 --> 01:21:04.350
and transform your data for you.

01:21:04.350 --> 01:21:05.580
RETO MEIER: That's excellent.

01:21:05.580 --> 01:21:08.207
Democratization of data has
been a big theme at Google.

01:21:08.207 --> 01:21:10.290
And we really believe that
if you want innovation,

01:21:10.290 --> 01:21:12.930
you have to democratize
access to data.

01:21:12.930 --> 01:21:15.798
Can you talk a little about
how BigQuery ML and AutoML

01:21:15.798 --> 01:21:17.340
are having an impact
towards us being

01:21:17.340 --> 01:21:18.423
able to achieve that goal?

01:21:18.423 --> 01:21:19.757
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, definitely.

01:21:19.757 --> 01:21:22.350
So both BQML and AutoML make
it easy for anyone, regardless

01:21:22.350 --> 01:21:23.960
of your machine
learning expertise,

01:21:23.960 --> 01:21:25.960
to build your own custom
machine learning model.

01:21:25.960 --> 01:21:28.980
So you don't need a lot of
machine learning expertise

01:21:28.980 --> 01:21:29.795
to get started.

01:21:29.795 --> 01:21:31.920
You just upload your data,
choose the type of model

01:21:31.920 --> 01:21:35.395
you want, and both BQML and
AutoML will handle the rest.

01:21:35.395 --> 01:21:37.270
RACHAEL TATMAN: And
AutoML, specifically, you

01:21:37.270 --> 01:21:40.970
don't need as much
labeled data as well.

01:21:40.970 --> 01:21:43.750
So I know the Natural
Language API--

01:21:43.750 --> 01:21:46.780
sorry-- the Natural Language
AutoML that just launched,

01:21:46.780 --> 01:21:50.090
you only need 100 label
examples per category

01:21:50.090 --> 01:21:52.000
for each label in your
categorical learning

01:21:52.000 --> 01:21:55.738
example, which is very little
data, especially when you're

01:21:55.738 --> 01:21:58.030
looking at, like, bird or
Elmer or these really, really

01:21:58.030 --> 01:22:00.790
enormous GPT-2 enormous
language models.

01:22:00.790 --> 01:22:03.730
So being able to get started
with relatively little data,

01:22:03.730 --> 01:22:06.550
smaller time investment, smaller
money investment for labeling

01:22:06.550 --> 01:22:09.793
is a really big movement
towards democratization.

01:22:09.793 --> 01:22:10.710
STEPHANIE WONG: Great.

01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:12.668
Sara, you'll actually be
talking about the path

01:22:12.668 --> 01:22:15.847
from Cloud AutoML to custom
model in your breakout session.

01:22:15.847 --> 01:22:17.680
Can you give us a preview
of the key factors

01:22:17.680 --> 01:22:20.227
when moving from AutoML
into custom ML models?

01:22:20.227 --> 01:22:21.560
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, definitely.

01:22:21.560 --> 01:22:23.210
So the goal of our breakout
session-- tomorrow,

01:22:23.210 --> 01:22:25.000
I'm going to be
speaking with Yufeng--

01:22:25.000 --> 01:22:27.220
is to teach people that may
not have built their own custom

01:22:27.220 --> 01:22:28.510
model before how to get started.

01:22:28.510 --> 01:22:29.950
Because at least
for me, the process

01:22:29.950 --> 01:22:32.010
of starting to learn how
to build a custom model

01:22:32.010 --> 01:22:32.830
is really intimidating.

01:22:32.830 --> 01:22:34.330
There's lots of
resources out there.

01:22:34.330 --> 01:22:36.050
It's hard to know
where to get started.

01:22:36.050 --> 01:22:37.990
So we're going to break it
down and talk about feature

01:22:37.990 --> 01:22:39.650
transformations,
feature engineering

01:22:39.650 --> 01:22:42.475
that you need to do to go
from AutoML to custom model

01:22:42.475 --> 01:22:43.600
and walk through some code.

01:22:43.600 --> 01:22:45.928
And there'll also be a
couple of live demos.

01:22:45.928 --> 01:22:47.470
RETO MEIER: Now,
Rachael, you'll also

01:22:47.470 --> 01:22:50.890
be leading a session tomorrow
using Google's data and AI

01:22:50.890 --> 01:22:52.480
technologies with Kaggle.

01:22:52.480 --> 01:22:54.730
What sort of things will you
cover and what part of it

01:22:54.730 --> 01:22:56.242
are you most excited about?

01:22:56.242 --> 01:22:57.700
RACHAEL TATMAN:
Oh, so we are going

01:22:57.700 --> 01:23:00.325
to give people a little tour of
Kaggle, because a lot of things

01:23:00.325 --> 01:23:01.277
have changed recently.

01:23:01.277 --> 01:23:03.610
Then we're also going to talk
about several integrations

01:23:03.610 --> 01:23:04.652
that we've made recently.

01:23:04.652 --> 01:23:06.790
And honestly, the one that
I'm most excited about

01:23:06.790 --> 01:23:09.130
is the ability to
take a Kaggle data set

01:23:09.130 --> 01:23:12.180
and launch it as a
Sheets instance--

01:23:12.180 --> 01:23:14.762
a Google Sheets instance-- with
a single click of a button.

01:23:14.762 --> 01:23:16.720
And one place where I
think this could actually

01:23:16.720 --> 01:23:19.760
be extremely useful for people
is for project management.

01:23:19.760 --> 01:23:21.653
So you're writing a
kernel and a notebook,

01:23:21.653 --> 01:23:24.070
and you're generating let's
say your hyperparameter search

01:23:24.070 --> 01:23:24.640
space.

01:23:24.640 --> 01:23:25.960
You save that as a data set.

01:23:25.960 --> 01:23:27.910
You launch it as
a sheet, and then

01:23:27.910 --> 01:23:31.360
you can assign action items,
link to other kernels,

01:23:31.360 --> 01:23:32.830
use traps, use
color coding, which

01:23:32.830 --> 01:23:33.970
you wouldn't want
to do in a CSV,

01:23:33.970 --> 01:23:36.290
but it could be really super
useful for project management.

01:23:36.290 --> 01:23:38.090
So I'm very excited
about that integration.

01:23:38.090 --> 01:23:38.400
RETO MEIER: Very cool.

01:23:38.400 --> 01:23:39.160
STEPHANIE WONG: Excellent.

